"92_winui_controls.mst — WG3 D3/D4: real Win32 controls, created by Smalltalk,
 notified through WG2's door, laid out by a Smalltalk layout object, serviced by
 WG3's drain (docs/sprints/sprint_wg3_detail.md, docs/win_gui_design.md §2.4a).

 Loaded via world/winui.list AFTER 91_winui_shell.mst, and the order is the
 sprint's own: `the drain lands before the first control exists`. 91 defines the
 whole flag-and-drain pass with four no-op hooks; this file overrides them. Part
 1 of the acceptance gate runs with this file's contents making no difference at
 all, which is the check that the ordering was honoured rather than claimed.

 ── Two classes, one job each ────────────────────────────────────────────
   WinLayout   PURE arithmetic. No handle, no Win32, no window — a client size
               and a DPI in, a Dictionary of rects out. That is what makes
               `testLayoutComputesRects` and `testLayoutScalesWithDpi` run
               headless in `world/tests/`, on a machine with no desktop, in
               parallel with everything else (WG0's Δ 11).
   WinControl  ONE child window: its HWND, its child id, its name, and the
               flag the door sets on it. It knows how to be created, moved,
               measured and asked whether it is still alive; it decides
               nothing about what its own notifications MEAN.

 ── Where a control's MEANING runs, and why it is not here ───────────────
 `WinShell>>serviceCommand:code:from:` and `serviceNotifyFrom:id:code:` are
 called from `drainPass`, never from a handler. The button's action sends two
 messages into two other controls, and Win32 delivers those synchronously — so
 running it in the door would be a VM entry nested inside a VM entry, which is
 the failure §2.4a exists to prevent and the one the Cocoa side paid for twice
 (silently failing closed, then corrupting the tier-link invariant once
 JIT-compiled).

 ── What measurement corrected in this file's own plan ───────────────────
 * A control's rect is asked of `GetWindowRect`, in SCREEN coordinates, and
   converted to client coordinates by subtracting the client area's own screen
   origin. `GetClientRect` on a child answers its size at (0,0) and says
   nothing about where it is, which is the number a layout test actually wants.
 * `SysListView32` does not exist until `InitCommonControlsEx` has run — and
   its failure mode is `CreateWindowExW` answering NULL, not an error anyone
   can read. `ensureCommonControls` is therefore checked, not assumed."

"── WinLayout ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 D4's layout object: a vertical stack of bands, computed in DIPs and scaled
 once at the end.

 The 'scaled once at the end' is the whole of why this class exists rather than
 a method that multiplies as it goes. WG1 established the environment's DPI
 contract as an EQUALITY (`client = DIP * dpi/96`), and a layout that scaled
 each band as it accumulated would round each one separately — so the same
 layout at 144 dpi would not be 1.5x the layout at 96, it would be 1.5x plus a
 pixel here and there. `testLayoutScalesWithDpi` asserts the exact thing, which
 is only assertable because the arithmetic is done in one space and converted in
 one step."
Object subclass: WinLayout [
    | bands margin |

    WinLayout class >> new ^ <WinLayout> [ ^super new initLayout ]

    WinLayout class >> shell ^ <WinLayout> [
        "The layout WG3's window actually wears: a command band at the top, a
         one-line status field under it, and a report-mode list filling the
         rest. Three bands is the smallest arrangement that exercises what D4
         has to get right — a fixed band, a second fixed band whose position
         depends on the first, and a filler whose SIZE depends on both."
        ^self new
            add: #button height: 32;
            add: #status height: 26;
            fill: #list;
            yourself
    ]

    initLayout [
        bands := OrderedCollection new.
        margin := 10.
        ^self
    ]

    margin ^ <Integer> [ ^margin ]
    margin: n <Integer> [ margin := n. ^self ]
    bandNames ^ <OrderedCollection> [ ^bands collect: [ :b | b at: 1 ] ]

    add: aName <Symbol> height: h <Integer> [
        "A fixed band, h DIPs tall, full width between the margins."
        bands add: (Array with: aName with: h).
        ^self
    ]

    fill: aName <Symbol> [
        "The band that takes whatever is left. At most one, and it must be
         last — the arithmetic below depends on both, and a layout object that
         let you write something it cannot compute is a worse object than one
         that refuses."
        bands do: [ :b | (b at: 2) isNil ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'WinLayout: a layout may have only one fill band (already ' ,
                (b at: 1) printString , ')' ] ].
        bands add: (Array with: aName with: nil).
        ^self
    ]

    rectsIn: clientWidth <Integer> by: clientHeight <Integer> dpi: dpi <Integer> ^ <Dictionary> [
        "name -> #(x y width height), in PIXELS, for a client area of
         clientWidth x clientHeight pixels at dpi.

         Computed entirely in DIP space and scaled ONCE, which is what makes
         the same layout at two DPIs exactly proportional."
        | dipW dipH out y avail fillHeight |
        dipW := clientWidth * 96 // dpi.
        dipH := clientHeight * 96 // dpi.
        out := Dictionary new.
        avail := dipH - (margin * (bands size + 1)).
        fillHeight := avail.
        bands do: [ :b | (b at: 2) isNil ifFalse: [ fillHeight := fillHeight - (b at: 2) ] ].
        fillHeight < 0 ifTrue: [ fillHeight := 0 ].
        y := margin.
        bands do: [ :b |
            | h |
            h := (b at: 2) isNil ifTrue: [ fillHeight ] ifFalse: [ b at: 2 ].
            out at: (b at: 1) put: (Array
                with: margin * dpi // 96
                with: y * dpi // 96
                with: (dipW - (margin * 2)) * dpi // 96
                with: h * dpi // 96).
            y := y + h + margin ].
        ^out
    ]

    rectFor: aName <Symbol> in: w <Integer> by: h <Integer> dpi: dpi <Integer> ^ <Array> [
        ^(self rectsIn: w by: h dpi: dpi) at: aName ifAbsent: [
            self error: 'WinLayout: no band named ' , aName printString ]
    ]
]

"── WinControl ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 One child window. WG0's WinRef is the honest home for a HANDLE; this is the
 honest home for a handle that has an IDENTITY — a child id Windows quotes back
 in WM_COMMAND's wParam and in WM_NOTIFY's NMHDR.idFrom, which is how a
 notification finds its way from four words of message to an object."
Object subclass: WinControl [
    | handle id name klass flag notifyCount lastCode |

    WinControl class >> name: aName <Symbol> class: aClass <String> text: aText <String>
                        style: style <Integer> exStyle: exStyle <Integer>
                        id: anId <Integer> in: parent <Integer> ^ <WinControl> [
        "CreateWindowExW against a SYSTEM window class — `BUTTON`, `EDIT`,
         `SysListView32` — with WS_CHILD and the caller's id in the `menu`
         slot, which is where a child window's id lives.

         Creation SENDS messages (WM_CREATE, WM_SIZE, WM_PARENTNOTIFY), so this
         must be called at open time or from the drain and never from a doit:
         from a doit those arrive at a VM that is already inside an entry and
         the busy guard declines them, which is correct but is not what a
         caller who expected them would think happened."
        | hwnd |
        (parent isNil or: [ parent = 0 ]) ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'WinControl ' , aName printString ,
                ': there is no parent window — send `WinShell openMain` first' ].
        hwnd := WinApi
            createWindowExStyle: exStyle
            class: (WinArena nativeUtf16: aClass)
            title: (WinArena nativeUtf16: aText)
            style: style
            x: 0 y: 0 width: 10 height: 10
            parent: parent
            menu: anId
            instance: WinApi moduleHandle
            param: 0.
        (hwnd isNil or: [ hwnd = 0 ]) ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'WinControl: CreateWindowExW(' , aClass , ') for ' ,
                aName printString , ' answered NULL (GetLastError ' ,
                WinApi lastError printString ,
                (aClass = 'SysListView32'
                    ifTrue: [ '; is InitCommonControlsEx done?' ]
                    ifFalse: [ '' ]) , ')' ].
        ^self new setHandle: hwnd id: anId name: aName class: aClass
    ]

    setHandle: h <Integer> id: anId <Integer> name: aName <Symbol> class: aClass <String> [
        handle := h. id := anId. name := aName. klass := aClass.
        flag := false. notifyCount := 0.
        ^self
    ]

    handle ^ <Integer> [ ^handle ]
    id ^ <Integer> [ ^id ]
    name ^ <Symbol> [ ^name ]
    windowClass ^ <String> [ ^klass ]
    lastCode ^ <Object> [ ^lastCode ]
    notifyCount ^ <Integer> [ ^notifyCount ]

    isAlive ^ <Boolean> [
        "WG1's Δ 2, inherited without argument: a remembered HWND is not a
         window. Asked of Win32 every time."
        (handle isNil or: [ handle = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ^WinApi asBool: (WinApi isWindow: handle)
    ]

    ref ^ <WinRef> [ ^WinRef onHandle: handle ]

    "── the door's half: a flag, set in microseconds ─────────────────────"

    flagged ^ <Boolean> [ ^flag == true ]
    flag [ flag := true. ^self ]
    clearFlag [ flag := false. ^self ]

    noteNotify: aCode <Integer> [
        notifyCount := notifyCount + 1.
        lastCode := aCode.
        ^self
    ]

    "── the drain's half: everything that touches Win32 ──────────────────"

    moveTo: aRect <Array> ^ <Boolean> [
        "One SetWindowPos per control per pass. DeferWindowPos exists for the
         flicker a multi-control move can cause and D4 says to reach for it `if
         measurement says the flicker warrants it` — measurement did not: three
         controls on a window whose parent has CS_HREDRAW|CS_VREDRAW redraw
         once either way, and a begin/end pair would add a handle whose failure
         mode is a silently un-laid-out window."
        self isAlive ifFalse: [ ^false ].
        ^WinApi asBool: (WinApi
            setWindowPos: handle
            after: 0
            x: (aRect at: 1)
            y: (aRect at: 2)
            width: (aRect at: 3)
            height: (aRect at: 4)
            flags: ((WinApi constant: 'SWP_NOZORDER') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'SWP_NOACTIVATE')))
    ]

    screenRect ^ <Array> [
        "#(left top right bottom) in SCREEN coordinates — what GetWindowRect
         answers for a child, and the only rect Win32 will tell you about a
         child's POSITION. `GetClientRect` on a child answers its size at
         (0,0), which is a different question."
        self isAlive ifFalse: [ ^self error: 'WinControl ' , name printString , ': no window' ].
        (WinApi asBool: (WinApi getWindowRect: handle into: WinShell rectScratch)) ifFalse: [
            ^self error: 'WinControl ' , name printString , ': GetWindowRect failed' ].
        ^WinArena rectAt: WinShell rectScratch
    ]

    clientRectIn: originArray <Array> ^ <Array> [
        "This control's rect as #(x y width height) relative to an origin —
         the same shape `WinLayout` answers, so the gate's item 7 is one
         equality between two independently produced Arrays."
        | r |
        r := self screenRect.
        ^Array
            with: (r at: 1) - (originArray at: 1)
            with: (r at: 2) - (originArray at: 2)
            with: (r at: 3) - (r at: 1)
            with: (r at: 4) - (r at: 2)
    ]

    setFont: hFont <Integer> [
        "Without this a control renders in the ancient bitmap System font even
         under visual styles v6 — the manifest chooses which comctl32 draws the
         control, not which font it draws with. Sent from open time, into a
         CHILD's own window proc, so it never touches this port's door."
        hFont = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        WinApi sendMessage: handle
               message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_SETFONT')
               wParam: hFont
               lParam: 1.
        ^self
    ]

    setText: aString <String> ^ <Boolean> [
        ^WinApi asBool: (WinApi setWindowText: handle text: (WinArena scratchUtf16: aString))
    ]

    text ^ <String> [
        | n |
        n := WinApi i32: (WinApi getWindowText: handle
                                buffer: WinShell textScratch
                                max: WinShell textBufferUnits).
        n < 0 ifTrue: [ n := 0 ].
        ^WinArena stringFromUtf16At: WinShell textScratch units: n
    ]

    send: msg <Integer> wParam: w <Integer> lParam: l <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "A message to THIS control's own (system) window proc. Never reaches
         this port's door: a BUTTON's or a list view's wndproc is comctl32's,
         and only the PARENT's is ours."
        ^WinApi sendMessage: handle message: msg wParam: w lParam: l
    ]

    printOn: aStream [
        aStream nextPutAll: 'a WinControl(', name printString, ' ', klass, ' id ';
                nextPutAll: id printString;
                nextPutAll: ' 16r'; nextPutAll: (handle isNil ifTrue: [ '0' ] ifFalse: [ handle printString: 16 ]);
                nextPutAll: ')'
    ]
]

"── WinShell, reopened ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 The four hooks 91 declares as no-ops, filled in. Nothing here is reachable
 unless this file is loaded, which is what lets `Part 1` of the gate prove the
 drain with no control in existence."
Object subclass: WinShell [
    <classVars: Controls ControlsById NextControlId Layout GuiFont
                ClickCount CommonControlsReady SelectRequest ListRowCount
                LastMeaningfulNotify ControlsEnabled RowScratch>

    "── the WG3 hooks, as delegations ────────────────────────────────────
     91 defines these as no-ops; this file supplies WG3's behaviour. They are
     one line each so that a LATER layer (93, WG4's shell) can override the
     hook and still reach this file's work by name — `super` cannot serve
     that purpose, because reopening a class creates no superclass link to
     its own previous definition, and a hook that could only be replaced
     wholesale would force every later sprint to copy this one."

    WinShell class >> buildControls ^ <OrderedCollection> [ ^self wg3BuildControls ]
    WinShell class >> applyLayout ^ <Integer> [ ^self wg3ApplyLayout ]
    WinShell class >> serviceCommand: anId <Integer> code: aCode <Integer> from: aHandle <Integer> [
        ^self wg3ServiceCommand: anId code: aCode from: aHandle
    ]

    "── the switch WG1's and WG2's gates need ────────────────────────────
     `tests_wg3.md` item 1 asks for WG2's entire gate to pass unchanged
     `with the drain installed and NO CONTROL CREATED`, and that phrase is
     load-bearing rather than incidental: a list view repaints, a repaint sends
     NM_CUSTOMDRAW, and WG2's gate counts every message that crossed the door.
     Controls also put white list-view pixels exactly where WG1's and WG2's
     gates read the window's background fill.

     So both older gates run with controls off, which is the configuration they
     were written against, and WG3's own gate runs with them on. The default is
     ON: this is the sprint that adds them."

    WinShell class >> controlsEnabled ^ <Boolean> [ ^ControlsEnabled ~~ false ]
    WinShell class >> controlsEnabled: aBoolean <Boolean> [ ControlsEnabled := aBoolean. ^self ]

    "── the ids ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A child id is a 16-bit number Windows quotes back in WM_COMMAND's wParam
     low word and in NMHDR.idFrom. Starting at 100 keeps clear of the standard
     dialog ids (IDOK is 1) that a system dialog would send if one ever became
     a child of this window."

    WinShell class >> firstControlId ^ <Integer> [ ^100 ]

    WinShell class >> controls ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        Controls isNil ifTrue: [ Controls := OrderedCollection new ].
        ^Controls
    ]

    WinShell class >> controlNamed: aName <Symbol> ^ <Object> [
        ^self controls detect: [ :c | c name == aName ] ifNone: [ nil ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> controlWithId: anId <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        ControlsById isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^ControlsById at: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> ownsControlId: anId <Integer> ^ <Boolean> [
        "WG12: a MENU id counts too. This answer decides whether the door
         returns 0 (handled) or falls through to DefWindowProcW, so a menu the
         shell owns but does not claim would work by accident today and break
         the moment anything else wanted that message."
        (self ownsMenuId: anId) ifTrue: [ ^true ].
        ^(self controlWithId: anId) notNil
    ]

    WinShell class >> layout ^ <WinLayout> [
        Layout isNil ifTrue: [ Layout := WinLayout shell ].
        ^Layout
    ]

    WinShell class >> clickCount ^ <Integer> [ ^ClickCount isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ ClickCount ] ]
    WinShell class >> listRowCount ^ <Integer> [ ^ListRowCount isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ ListRowCount ] ]

    "── D6's other half: the class library, not the manifest ─────────────"

    WinShell class >> ensureCommonControls ^ <Boolean> [
        "InitCommonControlsEx registers `SysListView32` (and the rest of the
         ICC_ set asked for). Without it CreateWindowExW answers NULL and
         nothing else says why, which is why this is CHECKED.

         Distinct from the v6 manifest and neither substitutes for the other:
         the manifest decides WHICH comctl32 is loaded (v5's Windows-95 skin or
         v6's themed one), this decides which classes that comctl32 registers.
         A window can perfectly well have working, ugly controls."
        | icc |
        CommonControlsReady == true ifTrue: [ ^true ].
        icc := WinArena alloc: (WinApi sizeOf: 'INITCOMMONCONTROLSEX').
        WinArena u32At: icc + (WinApi offsetOf: 'INITCOMMONCONTROLSEX' field: 'dwSize')
                 put: (WinApi sizeOf: 'INITCOMMONCONTROLSEX').
        WinArena u32At: icc + (WinApi offsetOf: 'INITCOMMONCONTROLSEX' field: 'dwICC')
                 put: (((WinApi constant: 'ICC_LISTVIEW_CLASSES')
                        bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'ICC_TREEVIEW_CLASSES'))
                        bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'ICC_STANDARD_CLASSES')).
        CommonControlsReady := WinApi asBool: (WinApi initCommonControls: icc).
        CommonControlsReady ifFalse: [
            ^self error: 'WinShell: InitCommonControlsEx failed (GetLastError ' ,
                WinApi lastError printString , ') — SysListView32 will not register' ].
        ^true
    ]

    WinShell class >> guiFont ^ <Integer> [
        "DEFAULT_GUI_FONT is a stock object: never deleted, valid for the life
         of the process, and the same handle every time. A control that is not
         sent one renders in the bitmap System font — 1994's default, which
         visual styles do not override."
        GuiFont isNil ifTrue: [ GuiFont := WinApi getStockObject: (WinApi constant: 'DEFAULT_GUI_FONT') ].
        ^GuiFont
    ]

    "── D3: the controls ─────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> wg3BuildControls ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        "Called from `openMain`, at open time — never from a doit (Δ2) and
         never from a handler (§2.4a). Idempotent, because `cycle: 10` opens
         ten windows in one VM and a second call must build children for the
         NEW window rather than hand back handles belonging to a destroyed one."
        | child |
        self ensureOpen: 'buildControls'.
        self controlsEnabled ifFalse: [
            Controls := OrderedCollection new.
            ControlsById := Dictionary new.
            ^Controls ].
        self ensureCommonControls.
        Controls := OrderedCollection new.
        ControlsById := Dictionary new.
        NextControlId := self firstControlId.

        child := self
            addControl: #button
            class: 'BUTTON'
            text: 'Do It'
            style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_VISIBLE'))
                bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'WS_TABSTOP')
                    bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'BS_DEFPUSHBUTTON')))
            exStyle: 0.

        child := self
            addControl: #status
            class: 'EDIT'
            text: 'clicks: 0'
            style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_VISIBLE'))
                bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'WS_BORDER')
                    bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'ES_AUTOHSCROLL')))
            exStyle: 0.

        child := self
            addControl: #list
            class: 'SysListView32'
            text: ''
            style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_VISIBLE'))
                bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'LVS_REPORT')
                    bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'LVS_SINGLESEL')
                        bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'LVS_SHOWSELALWAYS'))))
            exStyle: 0.
        self buildListColumns: child.
        self addListRow: 'the drain' detail: 'flag, then work'.
        self addListRow: 'the door' detail: 'record and return'.
        ^Controls
    ]

    WinShell class >> addControl: aName <Symbol> class: aClass <String> text: aText <String>
                       style: style <Integer> exStyle: exStyle <Integer> ^ <WinControl> [
        | c |
        c := WinControl
            name: aName class: aClass text: aText
            style: style exStyle: exStyle
            id: NextControlId in: Hwnd.
        c setFont: self guiFont.
        Controls add: c.
        ControlsById at: NextControlId put: c.
        NextControlId := NextControlId + 1.
        ^c
    ]

    "── the list view's own content ──────────────────────────────────────
     Struct-by-layout, at winkb-QUERIED offsets, exactly as WNDCLASSW is built
     in 91 — nothing here transcribes a member position."

    WinShell class >> buildListColumns: aControl <WinControl> [
        | col |
        col := WinArena alloc: (WinApi sizeOf: 'LVCOLUMNW').
        self setColumn: col text: 'what' width: 160 index: 0 in: aControl.
        self setColumn: col text: 'it does' width: 260 index: 1 in: aControl.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> setColumn: colPtr <Integer> text: aString <String> width: w <Integer> index: i <Integer> in: aControl <WinControl> [
        WinArena u32At: colPtr + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVCOLUMNW' field: 'mask')
                 put: (((WinApi constant: 'LVCF_TEXT') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'LVCF_WIDTH'))
                       bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'LVCF_SUBITEM')).
        WinArena u32At: colPtr + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVCOLUMNW' field: 'cx') put: w.
        WinArena u32At: colPtr + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVCOLUMNW' field: 'iSubItem') put: i.
        WinArena i64At: colPtr + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVCOLUMNW' field: 'pszText')
                 put: (WinArena nativeUtf16: aString).
        aControl send: (WinApi constant: 'LVM_INSERTCOLUMNW') wParam: i lParam: colPtr.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> addListRow: aString <String> detail: aDetail <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "One row, appended. Answers the item index Win32 assigned, or -1.

         Called from `buildControls` at open time and from the button's action
         IN THE DRAIN — never from a handler, because LVM_INSERTITEMW makes the
         list view notify its parent, and a notification arriving inside a
         handler is the nesting this whole architecture avoids."
        "The LVITEMW and both strings are REUSED, and both choices are the
         stress row's ('the arena does not grow without bound'). The arena
         never reclaims, so a fresh 88-byte struct per row would be a slow
         leak with a cliff at the end of the region — and `nativeUtf16:`
         memoises by VALUE, so 200 distinct row labels would be 200 permanent
         allocations. LVM_INSERTITEMW and LVM_SETITEMTEXTW both COPY the text
         before they return, which is exactly the contract `scratchUtf16:`
         asks its callers to satisfy."
        | item idx |
        RowScratch isNil ifTrue: [ RowScratch := WinArena alloc: (WinApi sizeOf: 'LVITEMW') ].
        item := RowScratch.
        WinArena u32At: item + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVITEMW' field: 'mask')
                 put: (WinApi constant: 'LVIF_TEXT').
        WinArena u32At: item + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVITEMW' field: 'iItem')
                 put: self listRowCount.
        WinArena u32At: item + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVITEMW' field: 'iSubItem') put: 0.
        WinArena i64At: item + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVITEMW' field: 'pszText')
                 put: (WinArena scratchUtf16: aString).
        idx := WinApi i32: ((self controlNamed: #list)
            send: (WinApi constant: 'LVM_INSERTITEMW') wParam: 0 lParam: item).
        idx < 0 ifTrue: [ ^-1 ].
        WinArena u32At: item + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVITEMW' field: 'iItem') put: idx.
        WinArena u32At: item + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVITEMW' field: 'iSubItem') put: 1.
        WinArena i64At: item + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVITEMW' field: 'pszText')
                 put: (WinArena scratchUtf16: aDetail).
        (self controlNamed: #list)
            send: (WinApi constant: 'LVM_SETITEMTEXTW') wParam: idx lParam: item.
        ListRowCount := self listRowCount + 1.
        ^idx
    ]

    WinShell class >> selectRequestFor: index <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "Build the LVITEMW that LVM_SETITEMSTATE reads, and answer its ADDRESS
         so the HOST can send the message.

         That division is WG2's Δ 3 made practical. A WM_NOTIFY that reaches
         Smalltalk must originate outside every VM entry, so the message cannot
         be sent from a doit — but the struct it points at can only be built by
         the side that owns the arena. So Smalltalk builds; the host's control
         drain sends. The address is stable arena storage, allocated once."
        SelectRequest isNil ifTrue: [ SelectRequest := WinArena alloc: (WinApi sizeOf: 'LVITEMW') ].
        WinArena u32At: SelectRequest + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVITEMW' field: 'mask')
                 put: (WinApi constant: 'LVIF_STATE').
        WinArena u32At: SelectRequest + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVITEMW' field: 'iItem') put: index.
        WinArena u32At: SelectRequest + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVITEMW' field: 'state')
                 put: ((WinApi constant: 'LVIS_SELECTED') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'LVIS_FOCUSED')).
        WinArena u32At: SelectRequest + (WinApi offsetOf: 'LVITEMW' field: 'stateMask')
                 put: ((WinApi constant: 'LVIS_SELECTED') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'LVIS_FOCUSED')).
        ^SelectRequest
    ]

    "── D4: the layout, run BY THE DRAIN ─────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> wg3ApplyLayout ^ <Integer> [
        "Compute every rect from the settled client size, then one SetWindowPos
         per control. Answers how many moved.

         This runs from `drainPass` and from nowhere else that matters. Each
         SetWindowPos here SENDS messages — to the child (whose wndproc is
         comctl32's, not ours) and, for WM_WINDOWPOSCHANGED, back to us — and
         the ones that do reach our door find the depth counter at 1, because
         the drain took the guard. They are declined, and that is exactly
         right: a layout that provoked another layout would not terminate."
        | rects moved |
        self isOpen ifFalse: [ ^0 ].
        rects := self layout
            rectsIn: self clientWidth
            by: self clientHeight
            dpi: (Dpi isNil ifTrue: [ WinApi constant: 'USER_DEFAULT_SCREEN_DPI' ] ifFalse: [ Dpi ]).
        moved := 0.
        self controls do: [ :c |
            | r |
            r := rects at: c name ifAbsent: [ nil ].
            r isNil ifFalse: [ (c moveTo: r) ifTrue: [ moved := moved + 1 ] ] ].
        ^moved
    ]

    WinShell class >> expectedRects ^ <Dictionary> [
        "What the layout SAYS the controls should be, for a gate that compares
         it against what GetWindowRect says they ARE. Two independent
         productions of the same numbers — the same shape of cross-check
         `lastSizeMatchesClientRect` already uses."
        ^self layout
            rectsIn: self clientWidth
            by: self clientHeight
            dpi: (Dpi isNil ifTrue: [ WinApi constant: 'USER_DEFAULT_SCREEN_DPI' ] ifFalse: [ Dpi ])
    ]

    WinShell class >> clientOrigin ^ <Array> [
        "The client area's top-left in SCREEN coordinates, obtained by
         subtracting the client rect's size from the window rect the same way
         `applyDips` measures the frame — no ClientToScreen, no assumption
         about where DWM put the border.

         Δ: the naive version uses the window rect's own left/top, which is
         wrong by exactly the frame width on every window with a border, and
         wrong by a plausible-looking small number rather than by an error.

         Δ (measured, and worth a line because it cost the first run of gate
         item 7): every intermediate here is a NAMED TEMPORARY, not an inline
         expression. Smalltalk's binary operators are strictly left to right —
         `left + frame // 2` is `(left + frame) // 2`, not `left + (frame // 2)`
         — and the first version of this method produced 112 where 216 was
         right, which is a coordinate, not an error. Nothing about it looked
         wrong; only the layout comparison said so."
        | wr cr frameW clientW clientH border |
        wr := self windowRect.
        cr := self clientRect.
        frameW := (wr at: 3) - (wr at: 1).
        clientW := (cr at: 3) - (cr at: 1).
        clientH := (cr at: 4) - (cr at: 2).
        border := (frameW - clientW) // 2.
        ^Array
            with: (wr at: 1) + border
            with: (wr at: 4) - border - clientH
    ]

    WinShell class >> actualRects ^ <Dictionary> [
        "Where the controls actually are, in client coordinates, read back from
         Win32."
        | origin out |
        origin := self clientOrigin.
        out := Dictionary new.
        self controls do: [ :c | out at: c name put: (c clientRectIn: origin) ].
        ^out
    ]

    WinShell class >> layoutMatches ^ <Boolean> [
        "Gate item 7, as one equality. Every control's real rect equals the
         rect `WinLayout` computed for the settled client size."
        | want got |
        self isOpen ifFalse: [ ^false ].
        want := self expectedRects.
        got := self actualRects.
        self controls do: [ :c |
            | w g |
            w := want at: c name ifAbsent: [ nil ].
            g := got at: c name ifAbsent: [ nil ].
            (w isNil or: [ g isNil ]) ifTrue: [ ^false ].
            1 to: 4 do: [ :i | (w at: i) = (g at: i) ifFalse: [ ^false ] ] ].
        ^true
    ]

    "── the MEANING of a control message, run in the drain ───────────────"

    WinShell class >> wg3ServiceCommand: anId <Integer> code: aCode <Integer> from: aHandle <Integer> [
        "The button's action. Two SendMessageW's into two other controls and a
         list-view insertion — synchronous Win32 work, i.e. exactly what a
         message handler may not do — running here, one pass later, with the VM
         quiescent.

         `tests_wg3.md` item 6 asks for this to be proven by SMALLTALK state
         rather than by a Rust counter, so the click count is a class variable
         and the status field is read back out of Win32 with GetWindowTextW."
        | c |
        c := self controlWithId: anId.
        c isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        c flag.
        c name == #button ifTrue: [
            ClickCount := self clickCount + 1.
            (self controlNamed: #status)
                setText: 'clicks: ' , ClickCount printString.
            self addListRow: 'click ' , ClickCount printString
                 detail: 'serviced in drain pass ' , self drainPasses printString ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> serviceNotifyFrom: aHandle <Integer> id: anId <Integer> code: aCode <Integer> [
        "A WM_NOTIFY, one pass after it arrived — with its NMHDR pointer long
         since invalid, which is precisely why the door read the three fields
         into Integers before it returned."
        | c |
        c := self controlWithId: anId.
        c isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        c noteNotify: aCode.
        "NM_CUSTOMDRAW arrives once per PAINT of a themed list view — it is a
         drawing callback wearing a notification's clothes, and it is the only
         member of the storm class that reaches this layer at all. Recorded but
         not treated as news, so `lastMeaningfulNotify` answers the thing a test
         (or a user) actually asked about rather than whatever was drawn last."
        "WG6a: the Outliner fills a class's own rows when it is selected.
         TVN_SELCHANGEDW arrives as 4294966845 — the UNSIGNED form of -451,
         which is how every tree-view notification code arrives. Comparing it
         against a signed constant would silently never match and the tree
         would simply never populate, with nothing to see."
        (aCode = (WinApi constant: 'TVN_SELCHANGEDW')
            and: [ c name == self outlinerName ])
                ifTrue: [ self populateSelectedClass ].
        aCode = (WinApi constant: 'NM_CUSTOMDRAW') ifFalse: [
            LastMeaningfulNotify := Array with: aHandle with: anId with: aCode ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> lastMeaningfulNotify ^ <Object> [ ^LastMeaningfulNotify ]

    "── reporting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> controlReport ^ <String> [
        | s |
        self controls isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^'<none built>' ].
        s := WriteStream on: String new.
        self controls do: [ :c |
            s nextPutAll: c name printString;
              nextPutAll: '(id '; nextPutAll: c id printString;
              nextPutAll: (c isAlive ifTrue: [ ' live' ] ifFalse: [ ' DEAD' ]);
              nextPutAll: ' notifies '; nextPutAll: c notifyCount printString;
              nextPutAll: ') ' ].
        ^s contents
    ]

    WinShell class >> controlReportLine [
        Transcript showCr: 'WinControls: ' , self controlReport.
        Transcript showCr: '  layout wants ' , self expectedRects printString.
        Transcript showCr: '  win32 says   ' , self actualRects printString.
        Transcript showCr: '  matches      ' , self layoutMatches printString.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> buttonCentreInWindow ^ <Array> [
        "The button's centre, in WINDOW coordinates — which is the coordinate
         system `snap` captures in (`gui/src/shell/snap.rs` sizes its bitmap by
         GetWindowRect, WG2's Δ 6). Gate item 8 reads the PNG at this point and
         asserts it is not the unthemed button face."
        | wr br |
        wr := self windowRect.
        br := (self controlNamed: #button) screenRect.
        ^Array
            with: ((br at: 1) + (br at: 3)) // 2 - (wr at: 1)
            with: ((br at: 2) + (br at: 4)) // 2 - (wr at: 2)
    ]

    WinShell class >> buttonProbePointInWindow ^ <Array> [
        "A point one sixth of the way across the button and vertically centred
         — inside the fill, clear of the label. The CENTRE is the obvious probe
         and it is the wrong one: a push button's centre is where its text is,
         so a themed button and an unthemed button both answer 'the text
         colour' there and the pixel proves nothing about either."
        | wr br |
        wr := self windowRect.
        br := (self controlNamed: #button) screenRect.
        ^Array
            with: (br at: 1) + (((br at: 3) - (br at: 1)) // 6) - (wr at: 1)
            with: ((br at: 2) + (br at: 4)) // 2 - (wr at: 2)
    ]

    WinShell class >> unthemedButtonFace ^ <Integer> [
        "COLOR_BTNFACE as a COLORREF — what a button looks like WITHOUT the v6
         manifest. Asked of the system rather than transcribed, so the gate's
         'themed, by pixel' assertion compares two things this machine
         produced."
        ^WinApi getSysColor: (WinApi constant: 'COLOR_BTNFACE')
    ]
]
